\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Outline of Single-Equation Linear Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Endogeneity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Our focus is on the single-equation linear model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of explanatory variables, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{i}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{i1}\\
\vdots\\
x_{iK}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

However, we explicitly allow the unobservable error to be correlated with
 the explanatory variables, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(x_{i}u_{i})\neq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

When this is the case, OLS estimation of equation (1) generally results
 in inconsistent estimators of 
\emph on
all
\emph default
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat{\beta}_{OLS} & = & \left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}y_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \beta+\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}u_{i}\right)\nonumber \\
 & \begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array} & \beta+\left[E(x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}\left[E(x_{i}u_{i})\right]\neq\beta\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

We consider two notable causes of the endogeneity problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Omitted Variable
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider a simple single-equation linear model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(y|x,q)=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x+\beta_{2}w+\beta_{3}q
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 are scalars.
 Written in regression form, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x+\beta_{2}w+\beta_{3}q+u
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $u\equiv y-E(y|x,q)$
\end_inset

 by definition, which is equivalent to the strict exogeneity condition 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u|x,w,q)=0$
\end_inset

.
 We refer to 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 as the structural error.
 Note that strict exogeneity implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(xu)=0$
\end_inset

.
 Now, in this setup, 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is unobserved and correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $E(xq)\neq0$
\end_inset

, so running an OLS regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 will lead to inconsistent estimates of the population parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $(\beta_{0},\beta_{1},\beta_{2})$
\end_inset

.
 However, if we impose additional assumptions, it may still be possible
 to consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Let the dependent variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 denote future salary, the binary explanatory variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 takes on values 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,1)$
\end_inset

 and signfies college attendance, and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is the unobservable variable that represents individual ability.
 We must control for ability by trying to compare people with the same ability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Measurement Errors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Once again, consider a simple single-equation linear model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(y|x)=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is a scalar.
 Written in regression form we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x+u
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $u\equiv y-E(y|x)$
\end_inset

 by definition.
 We don't observe 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, but instead we observe 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}=x+v$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 denotes the measurement error.
 We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 If we run an OLS regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

, then we will suffer from the endogeneity problem.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y & = & \beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x+u\\
 & = & \beta_{0}+\beta_{1}(x^{*}-v)+u\\
 & = & \beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x^{*}+\left(u-\beta_{1}v\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left[x^{*}\left(u-\beta_{1}v\right)\right] & = & E\left[(x+v)(u-\beta_{1}v)\right]\\
 & = & 0+0+0-\beta_{1}\sigma_{v}^{2}\neq0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

which leads to an inconsistent estimator
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta}_{OLS} & = & \left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{*\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{*}y_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \left(\sigma_{x}^{2}+\sigma_{v}^{2}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{*}x_{i}\beta+N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{*}u_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \left(\sigma_{x}^{2}+\sigma_{v}^{2}\right)^{-1}\left(\beta\sigma_{x}^{2}\right)\\
 & = & \beta\frac{\sigma_{x}^{2}}{\sigma_{x}^{2}+\sigma_{v}^{2}}<\beta
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|{\displaystyle \lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}}\hat{\beta}\right|<\left|\beta\right|
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
IV Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the linear model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{i1}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{iK}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where one of the regressors of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a constant.
 We assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1},\ldots,x_{i,K-1}$
\end_inset

 are all exogenous regressors, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(x_{ij}u_{i})=0,\ j=1,\ldots,K-1
\end{equation}

\end_inset

However, suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 is an endogenous regressor, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(x_{iK}u_{i})\neq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

 are correlated.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We assume that we have an 
\series bold
instrumental variable (IV)
\series default
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

, which we denote 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i1}$
\end_inset

.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i1}$
\end_inset

 to be satisfactory IV, it must satisfy the following two conditions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Exogeneity:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(z_{i1}u_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We are unable to test this condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Relevance
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(z_{i1}x_{iK})\neq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This condition is testable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We should avoid a 
\series bold
weak IV
\series default
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i1}x_{iK})$
\end_inset

 is close to zero, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

 are only weakly correlated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Identification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The column-vector 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 contains all the exogenous variables, including exogenous regressors and
 instruments
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{i}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{i1}\\
\vdots\\
x_{i,K-1}\\
z_{i1}
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Pre-multiply the linear regression equation (4) by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
z_{i}y_{i}=z_{i}(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i})
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(z_{i}y_{i}) & = & E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})\beta\\
\beta & = & \left[E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(z_{i}y_{i})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, if 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 has full rank, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is identified, and we have the 
\series bold
IV estimator
\series default

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}_{IV}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}y_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
1.
 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is salary; 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is education, which can be correlated with an omitted variable, such as
 ability; 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is quarter of birth
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
2.
 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is GPA; 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is alcohol consumption; 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is the regional price of alcohol.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Relevance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can't use an instrument that is a linear combination of the exogenous
 regressors because it would not provide any additional information, and
 it and cause perfect collinearity.
 We need to find an instrument that adds information regarding the endogeous
 regressor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the 
\series bold
reduced form (RF) equation
\series default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{iK}=\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}x_{i1}+\cdots+\delta_{K-1}x_{i,K-1}+\theta_{1}z_{i1}+v_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This is just the linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

 onto all of the exogenous variables.
 
\series bold
Relevance
\series default
 requires that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 This can be thought of as a stronger condition than the one listed above.
 The RF regression is not necessarily related to the model, and it does
 not need to have a linear conditional expectation because we can always
 obtain the 
\series bold
linear projection
\series default
 
\series bold
(LP)
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
2SLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
One Endogenous Regressor
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we have multiple IVs for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i1},\ldots,z_{iM}$
\end_inset

.
 So,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{i}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{i1}\\
\vdots\\
x_{i,K-1}\\
z_{i1}\\
\vdots\\
z_{iM}
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The procedure of 
\series bold
2SLS 
\series default
finds a linear combination of the exogenous variables that is most highly
 correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the following linear projection
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{iK}=\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}x_{i1}+\cdots+\delta_{K-1}x_{i,K-1}+\theta_{1}z_{i1}+\cdots+\theta_{M}z_{iM}+v_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

 onto the exogenous space, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{iK}$
\end_inset

, will be used as the instrument for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

.
 Pre-multiply equation (4) by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

, which contains the exogenous regressors and the linear projection 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{iK}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{x}_{i}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{i1}\\
\vdots\\
x_{i,K-1}\\
\hat{x}_{iK}
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Then, the 
\series bold
2SLS estimator
\series default
 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat{\beta}_{2SLS} & = & \left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{x}_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{x}_{i}y_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}\hat{x_{i}}\hat{x}_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}\hat{x}_{i}y_{i}}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

The second equation gives the name of 2SLS.
 The steps for 2SLS are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Reduced form regression: Run OLS regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 to get 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{iK}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Run OLS regression on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1},\ldots,x_{i,K-1},\hat{x}_{iK}$
\end_inset

.
 We then obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

, which is a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two important notes to remember in regards to 2SLS:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
All exogenous regressors should be included in the first stage regression.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The OLS standard errors reported with the second stage regression are incorrent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
General Setup
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the linear model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of explanatory variables that contains a constant.
 Suppose that at least one of the regressors is endogenous, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(x_{i}u_{i})\neq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Consequently, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is no longer a structural error.
 Assume we have we have a 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of IVs 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $L\geq K$
\end_inset

.
 The model is 
\series bold
just-identified
\series default
 when 
\begin_inset Formula $L=K$
\end_inset

, and it is 
\series bold
over-identified
\series default
 when 
\begin_inset Formula $L>K$
\end_inset

.
 Remember that 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 must satisfy two requirements:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Exogeneity:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(z_{i}u_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Relevance:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{rank}\ E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})=K
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the case with only one endogenous regressor, condition (20) is equivalent
 to at least one 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{j}\neq0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,M$
\end_inset

 in the linear projection
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{iK}=\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}x_{i1}+\cdots+\delta_{K-1}x_{i,K-1}+\theta_{1}z_{i1}+\cdots+\theta_{M}z_{iM}+v_{i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Linear Projections
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can always write the linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 onto 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{i}^{*\prime}=z_{i}^{\prime}\theta
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times K$
\end_inset

.
 Now, write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{i}^{\prime}=x_{i}^{*\prime}+r_{i}^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}r_{i}^{\prime})=0$
\end_inset

 and so 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}r_{i}^{\prime})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
x_{1i} & \cdots & x_{Ki}\end{array}\right]=z_{i}^{\prime}\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\theta_{1} & \cdots & \theta_{K}\end{array}\right]+\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
r_{1i} & \cdots & r_{Ki}\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{j}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ji}=z_{i}^{\prime}\theta_{j}+r_{ij}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,K$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Multiplying both sides of equation (22) by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 and taking expectations, we get
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})\theta=E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}r_{i}^{\prime})=E\left[z_{i}(x_{i}^{\prime}-z_{i}^{\prime}\theta)\right]=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\theta=\left[E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\theta_{j}=\left[E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(z_{i}x_{ij})
\]

\end_inset

The 
\series bold
analogy principle
\series default
 for choosing an estimator says to turn the population problem into its
 sample counterpart.
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\theta}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\theta}_{j}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}x_{ij}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a result,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{x}_{i}^{\prime}=z_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\theta}=z_{i}^{\prime}\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Deriving the 2SLS Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Using linear projections, there is a simple way to see how the relevance
 and exogeneity assumptions identify 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 First, assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular, we can always write the linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 onto 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*\prime}=z_{i}^{\prime}\theta$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\left[E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Since each column of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi$
\end_inset

 can be consistently esitmated by regressing the appropriate element of
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 onto 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, for the purposes of identification of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, we can treat 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 as known.
 Write 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}=x_{i}^{*\prime}+r_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}r_{i}^{\prime})=0$
\end_inset

 and so 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}r_{i}^{\prime})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Now, the 2SLS estimator is effectively the IV estimator using instruments
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Multiplying equation (17) by 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, taking expectations, and rearranging gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{\prime})\beta=E(x_{i}^{*}y_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is identified by 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=\left[E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(x_{i}^{*}y_{i})$
\end_inset

 provided 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular.
 But
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{\prime})=\theta^{\prime}E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})=E(x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})\left[E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})
\]

\end_inset

and this matrix is nonsingular if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 has rank 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

; that is, if and only if Assumption 2SLS.2b holds.
 If 2SLS.2b fails, then 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is singular and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is not identified.
 (Note that, because 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}=x_{i}^{*\prime}+r_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}r_{i}^{\prime})=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{\prime})=E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{*\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 So 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is identified if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{*\prime})=K$
\end_inset

.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 2SLS estimator can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}=\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}y_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We have the following consistency result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Equality of the Two Forms for the 2SLS Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Remember, the 2SLS estimator can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{x}_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{x}_{i}y_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

(IV interpretation), or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}=\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}\hat{x_{i}}\hat{x}_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}\hat{x}_{i}y_{i}}\right)
\]

\end_inset

(2SLS procedure).
 We now show that these two forms are equal.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta}_{2SLS} & = & \left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}\hat{x_{i}}x_{i}^{\prime}}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}\hat{x}_{i}y_{i}}\right)\\
 & = & \left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{\theta}^{\prime}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}\hat{\theta}^{\prime}z_{i}y_{i}}\right)\\
 & = & \left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}y_{i}\right)\right]\\
 & = & \left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\\
 &  & \times\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}y_{i}\right)\right]\\
 & = & \left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{\theta}^{\prime}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\theta}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{\theta}z_{i}y_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}\hat{x_{i}}\hat{x}_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}\hat{x}_{i}y_{i}}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Asymptotics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now summarize the asymptotic results for 2SLS in a single-equation model
 with perhaps several endogenous varialbes among the explanatory variables.
 The two assumptions needed for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 to be indentified, and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}$
\end_inset

 to be consistent, are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 (a) rank 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})=L$
\end_inset

, (b) rank 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})=K$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $(L\geq K)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we do not specify where the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 come from, but any exogenous elemnts of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, including a constant, are included in 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Unless every element of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous, 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 will have to contain variables obtained from outside the model.
 Using linear projections, there is a simple way to see how the two assumptions
 lead to 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}$
\end_inset

 being a consistent estimator for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Consistency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now prove 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\beta$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat{\beta} & = & \left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}y_{i}}\right)\right]\nonumber \\
 & = & \beta+\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}u_{i}}\right)\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\beta+\left[\left(Ex_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left(Ez_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(Ez_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left[\left(Ex_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left(Ez_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(Ez_{i}u_{i}\right)\right]=\beta
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Asymptotic Normality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By the CLT, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}-\beta\right) & \begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array} & \left[\left(Ex_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left(Ez_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(Ez_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left[\left(Ex_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left(Ez_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\right]\cdot N\left(0,E\left(u_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right)\\
 & = & N\left(0,V_{R}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cdot N(0,B)=N(0,ABA^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{R}$
\end_inset

 is the robust variance matrix.
 It is estimated by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}_{R}$
\end_inset

, where the expectations in 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{R}$
\end_inset

 are replaced by sample averages.
 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(u_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)$
\end_inset

 is estimated by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{u}_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}=y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now consider two cases that simply the asymptotic variance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Homoskedasticity Assumption:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left(u_{i}^{2}|z_{i}\right)=E(u_{i}^{2})=\sigma^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

By the law of iterated expectations, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}^{2}|z_{i})=E(u_{i}^{2})=\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Under this assumption, the non-robust variance matrix is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V_{NR}=\sigma^{2}\left[\left(Ex_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left(Ez_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(Ez_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The model is just-identified, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $(K=L)$
\end_inset

.
 Then, the asymptotic variance becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V_{R}=\left[\left(Ex_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left(Eu_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(Ez_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Hypothesis Testing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the following null hypothesis
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H_{0}:R\beta=\gamma
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $q\times K$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $q\times1$
\end_inset

.
 In the previous subsection, we showed
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}-\beta)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N(0,V_{R})
\]

\end_inset

Under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{N}(R\hat{\beta}-\gamma)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N(0,RV_{R}R^{\prime})
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the Wald Test is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sqrt{N}(R\hat{\beta}-\gamma)^{\prime}(R\hat{V}_{R}R^{\prime})^{-1}\sqrt{N}(R\hat{\beta}-\gamma)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\chi_{q}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $K=3$
\end_inset

, so we have and 
\begin_inset Formula $(\beta_{1,}\beta_{2},\beta_{3})$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose the null hypothesis is 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\beta_{2}=\beta_{3}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $R=(0,1,-1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Why the Second-Stage Standard Errors are Incorrect
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the 2SLS procedure, there are two types of residuals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The 2SLS residuals
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{u}_{i}=y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

consistently estimate
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{u_{i}}^{2}\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\sigma^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The second-stage residuals
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\tilde{u}_{i} & = & y_{i}-\hat{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}\\
 & = & y_{i}-\left(\hat{x}_{i}-x_{i}+x_{i}\right)^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}\\
 & = & \hat{u}_{i}-(\hat{x}_{i}-x_{i})^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{u}_{i}^{2}\right) & = & N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{u}_{i}^{2}+N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}\left[(\hat{x}_{i}-x_{i})^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}\right]^{2}-2N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{u}_{i}(\hat{x}_{i}-x_{i})^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}}\\
 & \begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array} & \sigma_{u}^{2}+\beta^{2}\sigma_{v}^{2}-2\sigma_{uv}\beta
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}\sim(0,\sigma_{u}^{2})$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{i}=z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+v_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}\sim(0,\sigma_{v}^{2})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}=x_{i}-\hat{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, under endogeneity, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}v_{i})=\sigma_{uv}\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
GMM Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Moment Conditions Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the single-equation linear model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i},\ i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of explanatory regressors that contains a constant.
 Suppose
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(x_{i}u_{i})\neq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that at least one regressor is endogenous.
 Assume we have a 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of IVs 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $L\geq K$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(z_{i}u_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Note that when 
\begin_inset Formula $L=K$
\end_inset

 the model is just identified, and when 
\begin_inset Formula $L>K$
\end_inset

 the model is over-identified.
 To alleviate the endogeneity problem, we have three possible solutions:
 IV estimation, 2SLS, and GMM estimation.
 Note that the IV and 2SLS estimators are both special cases under the class
 of GMM estimators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From the set of moment conditions (39), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left[z_{i}(y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right] & = & 0\\
E\left[g(w_{i},\beta)\right] & = & 0
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g(w_{i},\beta)=z_{i}(y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=(x_{i},y_{i},z_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Identificaiton
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From equation (40), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left(z_{i}y_{i}\right)-E\left(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\beta & = & 0\\
E\left(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\beta & = & E\left(z_{i}y_{i}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times K$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 , and 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(z_{i}y_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is identified if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 has rank 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

.
 This is known as the 
\series bold
rank condition
\series default
.
 A necessary condition for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 to be identified is for 
\begin_inset Formula $L\geq K$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We want the sample analogue of 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[g(w_{i},\beta)\right]=0$
\end_inset

, i.e,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}(y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

to be as close to zero as possible.
 Thus, we will minimize the squared Euclidean distance of equation (43).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We define the GMM estimator as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}_{GMM}=\arg{\displaystyle \min_{\beta}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}g_{i}(\beta)\right)^{\prime}\hat{W}}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}g_{i}(\beta)\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times L$
\end_inset

 weighting matrix that can be optimally chosen so as to minimize the asymptotic
 variance of the estimator and make it efficient.
 The GMM estimator satisfies the following first-order condition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
2\left(\frac{\partial{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}g_{i}^{\prime}(\beta)}}{\partial\beta}\right)\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}g_{i}(\beta})\right)=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Solving 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}g_{i}(\beta)}\right) & = & 0\\
\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}(y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)\right) & = & 0\\
\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}y_{i}-{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}\right) & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the expression for the GMM estimator is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}_{GMM}^{(\hat{W})}=\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\hat{W}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}y_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Consistency and Asymptotic Distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We assume
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \mbox{p}\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}}\hat{W}=W
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}_{GMM}-\beta\right) & = & \left[\left({\displaystyle N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\hat{W}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\hat{W}\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}u_{i}\right)\\
 & \begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\longrightarrow
\end{array} & \left[\left({\displaystyle E}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)W\left(Ez_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left(Ex_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)W\cdot N\left(0,Eu_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\\
 & = & N\left(0,V(W)\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V(W)=\left[\left({\displaystyle E}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)W\left(Ez_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left(Ex_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)W\left(Eu_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)W\left(Ez_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left[\left({\displaystyle E}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)W\left(Ez_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Asymptotic Distribution of the Optimal GMM Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*
The optimal weighting matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}_{op}$
\end_inset

 is such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
W=\left(Eu_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Formula $V(\hat{W})-V(\hat{W}_{op})$
\end_inset

 is positive semi-definite (p.s.d.) for any 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The optimal GMM estimator is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}_{GMM}=\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\hat{W}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\hat{W}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}y_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and its asymptotic distribution is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}-\beta)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\longrightarrow
\end{array}N\left(0,\left[(Ex_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})\left(Eu_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(Ez_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In general, the 2SLS estimator is non-optimal because 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{2SLS}=\left[Ez_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 However, it is optimal under conditional homoskedasticity (CH).
 By the law of iterated expectiations, conditional homoskedasticity, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{i}^{2}|z_{i})=E(u_{i}^{2})=\sigma^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Under one of these two homoskedasticity assumptions, the 2SLS estimator
 is the efficient GMM estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Also note that OLS is just a special case of GMM estimation where all the
 
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
regressors are exogeneous, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}=z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 When this occurs, then the asymptotic variance becomes
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\left(Ex_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left(Eu_{i}^{2}x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(Ex_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}=\left(Ex_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(Eu_{i}^{2}x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left(Ex_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

which is the asymptotic variance for the OLS estimator under heteroskedasticity.
 By the law of iterated expectations, conditional homoskedasticity, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{i}^{2}|x_{i})=\sigma^{2}
\]

\end_inset

implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{i}^{2}x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}\left(Ex_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Under one of these two homoskedasticity assumptions, the OLS estimator is
 efficient and its asymptotic variance becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}\left(Ex_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Two-Step Efficient GMM
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{W}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{u}_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}=y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\check{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is a preliminary consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, such as the 2SLS estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Use 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 in the GMM estimation to obtain the optimal GMM estimator.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

 given by (53) is known as the optimal weighting matrix for GMM estimation
 since
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{W}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\longrightarrow
\end{array}W=(Eu_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Other Choices of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}=I_{L}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The weighting matrix for 2SLS estimation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{W}=\left(n^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The weighting matrix for IV estimation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{W}=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
I_{K} & 0\\
0 & 0
\end{array}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, the IV and 2SLS estimators can be identified as special forms of the
 GMM estimator using a specific weighting matrix identfied by (55) and (56),
 respectively.
 If the model is just-identified, i.e, 
\begin_inset Formula $L=K$
\end_inset

, then the IV and 2SLS estimators are the same.
 If the model is over-identified, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $L>K$
\end_inset

, then we must use either the 2SLS estimator or the GMM estimator.
 If we assume homoskedasticity, then the 2SLS estimator is the efficient
 GMM estimator.
 If no homoskedasticity assumption is made, then the GMM estimator using
 the optimal weighting matrix given by (50) is efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Over-Identification Test
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The following results hold only when we have the optimal 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

.
 We wish to prove that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
J(\hat{\beta})=\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}g_{i}(\hat{\beta})\right)^{\prime}\hat{W}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}g_{i}(\hat{\beta})\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\longrightarrow
\end{array}\chi_{(L-K)}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

First off,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
g_{i}(\hat{\beta}) & = & z_{i}(y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta})\\
 & = & z_{i}(u_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}(\hat{\beta}-\beta))\\
 & = & z_{i}u_{i}-z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\left[\left(\sum x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\hat{W}\left(\sum z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left(\sum x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\hat{W}\left(\sum z_{i}u_{i}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Taking summation and multiplying both sides by 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum g_{i}(\hat{\beta}) & = & \left(I_{L}-\left(N^{-1}\sum z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left[\left(N^{-1}\sum x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\hat{W}\left(nN^{-1}\sum z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\right.\\
 &  & \left.\cdot\left(N^{-1}\sum x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\hat{W}\right)\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum z_{i}u_{i}\right)\\
 & = & B\cdot\bar{g}\\
\bar{g} & = & \frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}u_{i}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\longrightarrow
\end{array}N(0,W^{-1})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Hence,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
J=\bar{g}^{\prime}B^{\prime}\hat{W}B\bar{g}
\]

\end_inset

We can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B^{\prime}\hat{W}B=C^{\prime}MC
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}^{-1}=\hat{C}^{\prime}\hat{C}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $M=I_{L}-A(AA^{\prime})^{-1}A^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 (We just applied a triangular decomposition on 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{W}$
\end_inset

.) 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 is known as an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

annihilator
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 matrix, and it is idempotent, symmetric, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}(M)=L-K$
\end_inset

.
 Now
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
J=\bar{g}^{\prime}C^{\prime}MC\bar{g}
\]

\end_inset

Since
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
CW^{-1}C^{\prime}=C(C)^{-1}(C^{\prime})C^{\prime}=I_{L}
\]

\end_inset

then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C\bar{g}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\longrightarrow
\end{array}N(0,CW^{-1}C^{\prime})=N(0,I_{L})
\]

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
J=\left(N(0,I_{L})\right)^{\prime}M\left(N(0,I_{L})\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\longrightarrow
\end{array}\chi_{(L-K)}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

The 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

-test can be used to test the validity of additional IVs.
 Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:E\left(g_{i}\right)=0$
\end_inset

, i.e., all the IVs are valid.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Weak IVs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For simplicity, we will only consider the case of weak IVs for IV estimation,
 but the results in this subsection can be extended to the GMM framework.
 A weak IV occurs when the partial correlation, i.e., the relevance, between
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is small.
 This leads to two serious consequences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{IV}$
\end_inset

 is inconsistent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 The standard errors,
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{se}\left(\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}\right)$
\end_inset

, are not shrinking as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider the model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=\beta_{0}+x_{i}\beta_{1}+u_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a scalar and possibly endogenous.
 Suppose we have a single IV, 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 onto 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{i}=\gamma_{0}+z_{i}\gamma_{1}+v_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Suppose
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\gamma_{1}=\frac{c}{\sqrt{n}}
\]

\end_inset

which means that 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 is small.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left(x_{i}z_{i}\right)=E\left(z_{i}^{2}\right)\cdot\frac{c}{\sqrt{n}}
\]

\end_inset

and the sample analogue of 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}z_{i})$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
n^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}z_{i}=\frac{c}{\sqrt{n}}\cdot E\left(z_{i}^{2}\right)}
\]

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
(\hat{\beta}_{IV}-\beta) & = & \left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}u_{i}\right)\\
 & \begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\longrightarrow
\end{array} & \left[E\left(z_{i}x_{i}\right)\right]^{-1}E\left(u_{i}^{2}z_{i}^{2}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

which does not coverge in probability to 0.
 Also,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{n}(\hat{\beta}_{IV}-\beta)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\left[E(z_{i}x_{i})\right]^{-1}N\left(0,E(u_{i}^{2}z_{i}^{2})\right)
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Var}(\hat{\beta}_{IV})=\left[cEz_{i}^{2}\right]^{-1}E\left(u_{i}^{2}z_{i}^{2}\right)\left[cEz_{i}^{2}\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left(u_{i}^{2}z_{i}^{2}\right)=\sigma^{2}E(z_{i}^{2})
\]

\end_inset

then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Var}(\hat{\beta}_{IV})=\frac{\sigma^{2}E(z_{i}^{2})^{-1}}{c}
\]

\end_inset

which does not shrink as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Control Function Approach
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the single-equation linear model:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(x_{i}u_{i}\right)\ne0$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose we have a vector of IVs 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(z_{i}u_{i}\right)=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(z_{i}x_{i}\right)\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 We perform a linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 onto 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 in response to the endogeneity of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{i}=z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+v_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(u_{i}v_{i}\right)\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 The reason 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is endogenous is because 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

, the error term in the linear projection, is related to 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we setup the linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 onto 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u_{i}=\theta v_{i}+e_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(v_{i}e_{i}\right)=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\frac{E\left(u_{i}v_{i}\right)}{E\left(v_{i}^{2}\right)}$
\end_inset

.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}e_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Plugging equation (60) into equation (58) yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\theta v_{i}+e_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 is used to control for endogeneity.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a linear combination of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

, then we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}e_{i})=E\left[e_{i}(z_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+v_{i})\right]=0$
\end_inset

.
 However, 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 is unobserved, so we replace 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v_{i}}=x_{i}-z_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\gamma}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
Control Function (CF) estimator
\series default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{CF}$
\end_inset

, is obtained by regressing 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 It can be shown that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}=\hat{\beta}_{CF}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

